Distributed Web Infrastructure
===============================

Overview
--------
This setup uses three servers for www.foobar.com: 1 load balancer
(HAProxy) and 2 servers. Each of the 2 servers has its own web server
(Nginx), application server, app code, and MySQL database (set up as
Primary-Replica).

What got added, and why
-------------------------
- Load balancer (HAProxy): spreads incoming traffic across both
  servers so one server doesn't get overloaded, and removes the
  "one server handles everything" weak point.
- A 2nd server (web server + app server + code + database): gives
  backup - if one server goes down, the other keeps things running.
  It also lets you scale out (add more servers) instead of just up.
- Primary-Replica database setup: one database (Primary) handles all
  writes, the other (Replica) handles reads. This spreads out the
  work and gives you a backup copy of the data.

Load balancer algorithm
------------------------
It's set up with Round Robin: each new request goes to the next
server in line, then loops back to the first once it reaches the end.
That way traffic is spread evenly across both servers.

Active-Active vs Active-Passive
--------------------------------
This is Active-Active: both servers are live and handling traffic at
the same time.
In Active-Passive, only one server (active) handles traffic while the
other (passive) just sits there as backup, kicking in only if the
active one fails.
Difference: Active-Active uses both servers all the time (so
resources aren't wasted), while Active-Passive keeps one server idle
just in case (simpler, but that server sits unused most of the time).

Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) database setup
--------------------------------------------------
The Primary handles every write (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE). Those
changes then get copied over to the Replica(s). The Replica keeps an
updated copy of the data and usually handles reads, which takes load
off the Primary.

Primary vs Replica, from the app's point of view
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The app sends all writes to the Primary. It can send reads to either
the Primary or a Replica. The app needs to know which one is the
Primary (for writes) and which are Replicas (for reads) - you can't
write to a Replica.

Issues with this setup
--------------------------------
Where are the weak points (SPOFs)?
- The load balancer is one: if it goes down, no traffic gets through
  to either server.
- The Primary database is one too: if it goes down, no writes can
  happen until a Replica gets promoted to take over.

Security issues:
- No firewall: anything from the internet can reach the servers,
  nothing is filtering out bad traffic.
- No HTTPS: traffic between the user and the servers isn't encrypted,
  so data could be intercepted or messed with along the way.

No monitoring:
There's nothing watching server health, performance, or errors - so
problems might not get noticed until the site is already having
issues for users.
